the United States of America currently  has 40-year high inflation the last  print was at 8.6 percent we haven't seen  that since the 1980s and of course  there's many people who are asking  themselves how did we get here now  there's some things in the supply chain  that have been disrupted there's  obviously geopolitical issues like the  Russia Ukraine conflict but really most  of it was driven by undisciplined  monetary and fiscal policy as we got the  covid pandemic all of a sudden the  government and the Central Bank decided  they needed to step into the market they  went ahead and they dropped interest  rates to zero and they pumped liquidity  into every exotic corner of the  financial system that they could find  all of that loose monetary policy and  all of those loose fiscal policies ended  up handing tons and tons of free cash to  American which sounded like a great idea  at the time according to them but  unfortunately when you go ahead and  create so much free money and you just  hand it out to people it ends up driving  inflationary pressures higher and we get  that 8.6 inflation that we're now facing  today now the state of California they  didn't seem to have learned from this  past lesson they just announced a brand  new effort where they're gonna hand more  free cash directly to their citizens the  governor of California Gavin Newsom  states that millions of Californians  will now be receiving up to one thousand  and fifty dollars as part of a new  middle class tax rebate that's more  money in their pockets to help them fill  up their gas tanks and put food on the  table it's pretty insane that the  government thinks they have to give a  thousand dollars to these individuals  just to fill up their gas tank or to put  food on their table that's warning sign  number one if you start to look deeper  and deeper into what's going on here  what you can find is they're doing this  via some sort of tax rebate it's part of  a 17 billion dollar relief package and  about 9.5 billion of it is going towards  these inflation relief funds it'll go to  about 23 million California taxpayers  and those folks can receive anywhere  between two hundred dollars and one  thousand and fifty dollars but they may  not get it until early next year if we  then take a look single tax filers they  can receive anywhere between 200 and 350  dollars if somebody's making between 125  000 and a quarter of a million dollars  per year State of California give it 200  bucks hope that fills your gas tank and  put some food on the table if you look  at joint tax filers what you'll see here  is that if you make between a quarter of  a million and half a million dollars per  year well they'll give you four hundred  dollars again fill up your gas tank baby  and make sure you got food on the table  as we go and we take a look at these  policies what we are watching is that it  appears politicians do not learn even if  those history lessons are only two years  old handing out free money two citizens  in this manner simply drives higher and  higher inflation pressures what we need  instead is to allow the free market to  work rather than turn around and hand  out money to people instead what we  should be doing is we should be  incentivizing economic growth and we  should actually be allowing the free  market to work one thing that we do know  is certain is that high gas prices are  here and they don't appear to be going  away we also know that food the cost of  it to eat at home is up 12 over the last  12 months it sounds great when a  government says that they're going to  hand you free money it sounds amazing  when they say they're going to help you  fill up your gas tank or put food on  your table the problem is we're talking  about giving 400 dollars to households  that make between a quarter of a million  and half a million dollars per year this  is absolutely insane so the state of  California is going to spend 9.5 billion  dollars to hand out a couple hundred  dollars to individuals but at the end of  the day it doesn't solve the problem  what it does is it simply drives higher  inflationary pressures and it allows for  the problem to persist if you want to  recognize that inflation is a problem if  you want to recognize that your citizens  are suffering you need to think from  first principles and you need to solve  the pressures of inflation not simply  hand out more money because it makes it  sound good we're going into midterm  elections and don't think that there's  not a connection between election season  and Free Money Getting handed out some  people would even argue this is the  equivalent of literally trying to buy  votes now I'm not going to go that far  and say that's what they're doing but  what I am going to say is that this type  of effort sounds great in theory but it  absolutely gets destroyed when you get  into reality handing free money to  people in this manner is not effective  for actually solving the problem does it  give them a couple hundred bucks in  their pocket absolutely but if you're  not going to give them the money until  early the next year potentially and  you're also not going to solve the  actual first principles problem  then all you're doing is grandstanding  and you're wasting 9.5 billion dollars  to the California state budget just so  you can go on Twitter tweet about how  cool you are and how you're looking out  for the citizens at the end of the day  this doesn't help them nearly as much as  you think it does and it may even make  the problem worse
 

